WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:01.350
Part nine.

00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:03.340
The SQL Test Case Designer.

00:00:04.833 --> 00:00:07.606
Behind the Demo Books list is a SQL query.

00:00:07.606 --> 00:00:12.216
The SQL Test Case Designer turns that very query into a test case list.

00:00:12.433 --> 00:00:14.176
Open it from the Tools tab.

00:00:16.833 --> 00:00:20.028
It runs in its own tab, wide enough for the tables.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:21.450
Paste the query.

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:26.222
MySQL, PostgreSQL or SQL Server syntax all work.

00:00:26.233 --> 00:00:27.441
Click Analyze.

00:00:27.441 --> 00:00:30.834
It all runs in your browser, and nothing is sent anywhere.

00:00:31.167 --> 00:00:41.311
You get numbered test cases with a priority, a technique and sample data: equivalence partitions, boundary values, a decision table, NULLs and JOINs.

00:00:41.333 --> 00:00:46.199
Pick a case to see its condition, the values it uses, and the expected result.

00:00:51.667 --> 00:00:58.012
The Schema tab shows the tables and columns the tool worked out from the query, with primary and foreign keys.

00:00:58.033 --> 00:01:03.933
Click Diagram to see it drawn: each table is a box, and the line is the JOIN between them.

00:01:03.933 --> 00:01:08.639
Drag the boxes around, scroll to zoom, and click Fit to see it all.

00:01:11.833 --> 00:01:15.761
Then export it to CSV or JSON for your test management tool.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:20.646
Next, still the database, but this time real data during a test.
